    when_ru: "\u041f\u0435\u0440\u0435\u0432\u043e\u0434 \u0434\u043b\u0438\u043d\u043d\u043e\u0433\u043e \u0438\u043b\u0438 \u0441\u043f\u0435\u0446\u0438\u0430\u043b\u044c\u043d\u043e\u0433\u043e \u0442\u0435\u043a\u0441\u0442\u0430 (\u0434\u043e\u043a\u0443\u043c\u0435\u043d\u0442, \u0441\u0442\u0430\u0442\u044c\u044f, \u0434\u043e\u0433\u043e\u0432\u043e\u0440, \u0441\u0442\u0440\u043e\u043a\u0438 \u0438\u043d\u0442\u0435\u0440\u0444\u0435\u0439\u0441\u0430, \u0441\u0443\u0431\u0442\u0438\u0442\u0440\u044b), \u0433\u0434\u0435 \u0432\u0430\u0436\u043d\u044b \u0442\u0435\u0440\u043c\u0438\u043d\u044b, \u0442\u043e\u043d \u0438\u043b\u0438 \u0444\u043e\u0440\u043c\u0430\u0442. \u041d\u0435 \u0434\u043b\u044f \u0441\u043b\u043e\u0432\u0430 \u0438\u043b\u0438 \u043a\u043e\u0440\u043e\u0442\u043a\u043e\u0439 \u0444\u0440\u0430\u0437\u044b.",
    prompt: "PURPOSE: a translation a native-speaking specialist would sign off, faithful to meaning and natural to read.\n\nBEFORE TRANSLATING\n- Identify the source and target languages, the text type (legal, technical, marketing, UI, literary, subtitles, correspondence), the audience and the register. If the target variant matters (British or American English, Brazilian or European Portuguese, formal \"\u0412\u044b\" or informal \"\u0442\u044b\"), follow the user's choice or pick the obvious one and say which.\n- Scan the whole text first and build a mental glossary of recurring terms, product names and abbreviations so each is translated the same way everywhere.\n\nTRANSLATING\n1. Translate meaning, not words: restructure sentences so they read as if originally written in the target language, without adding or dropping information.\n2. Keep names, brands, code, file paths, URLs, placeholders ({name}, %s, {{count}}), HTML/Markdown tags and numbers exactly as they are. Never translate inside code blocks unless the user asks to translate comments.\n3. Adapt conventions: date and number formats, units (keep the original in brackets when precision matters), quotation marks, punctuation and capitalisation rules of the target language.\n4. Terminology: use the established term in the field; for a term with no settled translation keep the original in brackets on first use.\n5. Idioms and humour: replace with an equivalent that works in the target culture; if none exists, translate the meaning plainly.\n6. UI strings: keep them short (buttons are verbs), respect length limits, keep plural forms and gender agreement correct for every placeholder value.\n7. Legal and medical texts: precision beats elegance; never soften obligations (\"shall\", \"must\") or change modality.\n\nOUTPUT\nThe translation only, in the same structure and formatting as the source. After it, at most a few short notes: ambiguous passages and the interpretation chosen, terms kept in the original, and anything that could not be translated faithfully. For long documents offer a glossary of key terms.\n\nRULES\nDo not summarise, censor or \"improve\" the content unless asked. Flag likely errors in the source instead of silently correcting facts.",
