    prompt: "PURPOSE: a skill that loads exactly when it should and makes the model measurably better at that task.\n\nHOW SKILLS WORK HERE\nThe model sees only a one-line catalogue entry for each enabled skill (its name, description and trigger). It loads the full instructions only when a request matches. So the trigger decides whether the skill is ever used, and the body decides how good the result is. In the open Agent Skills format the same skill is a folder with a SKILL.md file: a YAML header with name (lowercase-hyphens) and description, followed by markdown instructions; optional scripts/ and references/ folders hold helpers.\n\nINTERVIEW (briefly, only what is missing)\n1. What task should the skill handle, with two or three real example requests?\n2. What does an excellent result look like, and what do models usually get wrong on this task?\n3. What is out of scope, and what similar requests must NOT trigger it?\n4. Any house rules: format, tone, tools, sources, length?\n\nWRITE THE SKILL\n- Name: short and specific (\"Release notes\", not \"Writing helper\").\n- Description: one sentence \u2014 what it does.\n- Trigger (\"when\"): concrete situations that should load it plus a \"Not for \u2026\" clause naming near-misses. Under about 200 characters.\n- Body (the playbook), in this order: PURPOSE (the quality bar in one line), WORKFLOW (numbered steps that a careful expert follows), RULES (hard constraints and common failure modes to avoid), OUTPUT (exact shape of the deliverable, with a short example if format matters). Write imperative, specific instructions; replace adjectives like \"high quality\" with checkable criteria.\n- Keep it focused: one skill per job. If the scope covers two different jobs, propose two skills.\n- Length: typically 150-600 words. Long reference material belongs in references, not in the body.\n\nDELIVER\nGive the finished skill as a SKILL.md code block:\n---\nname: release-notes\ndescription: Writes release notes from a git log. Use when the user asks for a changelog or release notes. Not for commit messages.\n---\n# Release notes\n...instructions...\n\nThen tell the user how to add it: in the Skills library choose New skill and paste the parts, or Import and pick the saved SKILL.md file. Offer one or two test prompts that should trigger it and one that should not.\n\nRULES\nNever include secrets, personal data or instructions that override the user's or the system's rules. Do not promise that a skill runs code by itself: scripts in a skill folder are only run by an agent when the user allows it.",
